{"surface": "inspector", "title": "Full text", "html": "<header class=\"hero\"><p class=\"kicker\">Full text</p><h2 class=\"title\">Stock Market News — November 19, 2026 — Morning Update — Last 12 Hours (Pacific Time)</h2><p class=\"ctx\"><a href=\"https://www.everhint.com/stock-market-news-november-19-2026-morning-update-last-12-hours-pacific-time/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">everhint.com</a> · read Nov 19, 2026</p></header><div class=\"page md\" data-md=\"[![EverHint Daily Stock Picks, Markets &amp; Sentiment](https://storage.ghost.io/c/15/2a/152a3d3b-1437-45a5-9ee6-e4ae107268d7/content/images/2025/10/logo-4.svg)](https://www.everhint.com)\n\n- [Home](https://www.everhint.com/)\n- [About](https://www.everhint.com/about/)\n- [FAQ](https://www.everhint.com/faqs/)\n- [Disclaimer](https://www.everhint.com/disclaimer/)\n- [Privacy Policy](https://www.everhint.com/privacy/)\n\n[Sign in](#/portal/signin) [Subscribe](#/portal/signup)\n\n19 Nov 2026 9 min read [stock-market-news](https://www.everhint.com/tag/stock-market-news/)\n\n# Stock Market News — November 19, 2026 — Morning Update — Last 12 Hours (Pacific Time)\n\nAnthropic&#x27;s record $81.3B IPO debut sparked a global AI and semiconductor rally, but enthusiasm faded as the Nasdaq turned negative and macro concerns weighed on markets. AMD tumbled 8-9% on short-seller concerns, while a cyberattack hit JPMorgan, Visa, and Mastercard vendors, offsetting gains from\n\n[Vladimir Shamgin](https://www.everhint.com/author/vladimir/)\n\n---\n\n## Executive Summary\n\nAnthropic&#x27;s Nasdaq debut dominated the session, raising a record $81.3 billion at $83/share and briefly hitting a $2 trillion market cap — before retreating nearly 9% from its opening high as the broader Nasdaq turned negative in choppy trade. The IPO ignited a global semiconductor and AI infrastructure rally in premarket, but that enthusiasm faded as macro concerns, a short-seller attack on AMD, and a cyberattack hitting JPMorgan, Visa, and Mastercard vendors weighed on sentiment. European markets closed broadly lower despite the AI excitement, while Asia saw sharp losses led by Taiwan&#x27;s 4.6% plunge.\n\n---\n\n## Sentiment Breakdown\n\n| Sentiment | Count | Percentage |\n| --------- | ----- | ---------- |\n| 🟢 Bullish | 98    | 39%        |\n| 🟡 Neutral | 42    | 17%        |\n| 🔴 Bearish | 110   | 44%        |\n\n**Net Sentiment: Mildly Bearish.** The Anthropic IPO generated genuine excitement and a broad AI/chip rally, but the enthusiasm failed to hold — Nasdaq turned negative, European markets closed in the red, Asia sold off sharply, and multiple individual stocks faced significant headwinds from earnings misses, short-seller reports, and macro risks.\n\n---\n\n## Top Market-Moving Headlines\n\n🟢 **IPO / AI** | **Anthropic raises record $81.3B, opens 34% above IPO price, briefly hits $2 trillion market cap**  \nMarket Impact: The largest IPO in history ignited a global AI and semiconductor rally; however, shares retreated nearly 9% from the opening high as the Nasdaq turned negative, tempering the euphoria.\n\n🔴 **IPO / AI** | **Nasdaq turns negative in choppy trade even as Anthropic surges in market debut**  \nMarket Impact: Despite the historic IPO, the tech-heavy Nasdaq flipped red, signaling that the broader market was unwilling to chase the AI narrative at current valuations.\n\n🔴 **Semiconductors** | **AMD shares slide 8-9% after short-seller Hunterbrook Capital flags China export concerns**  \nMarket Impact: Hunterbrook disclosed a short position citing potential US regulatory scrutiny over AMD&#x27;s China links, triggering one of the day&#x27;s sharpest single-stock selloffs in the chip sector.\n\n🟢 **Cybersecurity** | **Palo Alto Networks surges 6%+ after raising annual sales forecast; lifts sector peers**  \nMarket Impact: A clean earnings beat and raised guidance sent Palo Alto to session highs and pulled CrowdStrike (+6.4%), Zscaler (+4.3%), Okta (+4.9%), Rubrik (+5.1%), and Cloudflare (+7.7%) higher in premarket.\n\n🔴 **Cybersecurity / Financials** | **JPMorgan, Visa, Mastercard confirm third-party vendor cyberattack; customer data leaked**  \nMarket Impact: A ransomware group leaked data tied to thousands of customers across all three institutions; JPMorgan clarified the breach was vendor-side and unrelated to a separate earlier outage.\n\n🟢 **Semiconductors / AI Infrastructure** | **Global chip stocks rally on Anthropic IPO: Micron +5.2%, Western Digital +9%, Seagate +7.6%, Coherent +10.9%, CoreWeave +8.5%**  \nMarket Impact: Anthropic&#x27;s debut acted as a confidence signal for AI infrastructure spending, lifting storage, memory, and data center stocks broadly in premarket before gains partially faded.\n\n🟢 **M&amp;A / Banking** | **UniCredit and Germany reach broad agreement on Commerzbank takeover terms**  \nMarket Impact: The long-running deal cleared a major political hurdle; UniCredit shares rose 2% on the news, with CEO confirming job cuts will start at the top and HVB can assist.\n\n🔴 **Geopolitics / Energy** | **European gas surges on reports of impending US military action against Iran**  \nMarket Impact: Energy markets moved sharply on the geopolitical headline, adding a risk-off dimension to an already volatile session.\n\n🟢 **Pharma / M&amp;A** | **Insmed surges 16-18% after Bloomberg reports Sanofi is weighing acquisition**  \nMarket Impact: Shares were briefly halted by a volatility circuit breaker; Sanofi separately received FDA approval for a first-ever treatment for a rare neurological disorder, adding to its deal-making momentum.\n\n🟢 **Gaming** | **GTA VI launches globally to rave reviews; Take-Two set to shatter sales records**  \nMarket Impact: Sony rose 3% as PlayStation accounted for roughly three-quarters of first-day sales; paradoxically, Take-Two stock slipped 2.7% — a classic &quot;sell the news&quot; reaction after years of anticipation.\n\n🔴 **Retail / Consumer** | **Williams-Sonoma falls 8% on Q3 fiscal 2026 results; Dick&#x27;s Sporting Goods drops 3.9% on sell downgrade**  \nMarket Impact: Williams-Sonoma&#x27;s results disappointed; Dick&#x27;s was cut to Sell by Williams Trading, which also flagged a Jordan brand headwind at Foot Locker.\n\n🟢 **Retail** | **Walmart lifts annual targets ahead of holidays but warns of fuel cost pressure**  \nMarket Impact: The guidance raise is a positive read-through for consumer spending heading into the holiday season, though rising fuel costs could compress margins.\n\n🔴 **Taiwan / Semiconductors** | **Taiwan Weighted Index plunges 4.6% to six-month low; chip and PCB shares hit limit-down**  \nMarket Impact: The sharp selloff — driven by AI spending doubts and TSMC tightening China chip restrictions — undercut the Anthropic-driven optimism and weighed on global semiconductor sentiment.\n\n🟡 **Geopolitics / Tech** | **TSMC tightens restrictions on Chinese chip designers as new US rules close manufacturing loophole**  \nMarket Impact: The move signals escalating US-China tech decoupling and adds supply-chain uncertainty for Chinese AI hardware development.\n\n🔴 **Geopolitics / Tech** | **FCC votes to ban Chinese telecoms from direct access to US phone numbers; lawmakers push to block Chinese aircraft certification**  \nMarket Impact: Two separate actions in one session reflect a broadening US regulatory push against Chinese technology and infrastructure access.\n\n🟢 **AI / Cloud** | **Google selects Blue Origin&#x27;s New Glenn rocket to launch AI computing satellites in 2029**  \nMarket Impact: The contract is a win for Jeff Bezos&#x27;s rocket company and signals that hyperscalers are investing in space-based AI compute infrastructure.\n\n🔴 **Autos** | **Toyota recalls 314,029 US vehicles over Takata airbag rupture risk; Moody&#x27;s upgrades Toyota outlook to positive**  \nMarket Impact: The recall is a legacy liability issue; the Moody&#x27;s positive outlook upgrade on modest EBIT margin expansion provides a partial offset.\n\n🟡 **Labor / Industrials** | **US Steel and United Steelworkers reach tentative four-year labor deal**  \nMarket Impact: Removes near-term strike risk for US Steel; provides operational stability as the company navigates ongoing strategic uncertainty.\n\n🔴 **Consumer Staples** | **Coca-Cola slides 2.6% amid valuation-reset pressure**  \nMarket Impact: The move reflects broader defensive sector rotation and valuation concerns rather than a company-specific catalyst.\n\n🟡 **Retail / Earnings** | **Target Q3 beats on every line (adj. EPS $2.65); TJX closes up 8.05% day prior on strong results**  \nMarket Impact: Two strong consumer discretionary/off-price prints suggest the US consumer remains resilient heading into the holiday stretch, even as some retailers disappoint.\n\n---\n\n## Tickers in Focus\n\n| Ticker                       | Price / Change                                            | Context                                                                  |\n| ---------------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| ANT (Anthropic)              | Opened $111.50 (+34% vs $83 IPO); retreated ~9% from open | Record $81.3B IPO; Nasdaq debut; $2T peak valuation                      |\n| AMD                          | -8% to -9.1%                                              | Short-seller Hunterbrook Capital flagged China export concerns           |\n| PANW                         | +6% to +9.1%                                              | Beat on earnings; raised annual sales forecast                           |\n| CRWD                         | +6.4% (premarket)                                         | Cybersecurity sector rally on PANW results                               |\n| NET (Cloudflare)             | +7.7%                                                     | Cybersecurity sector rally                                               |\n| OKTA  