to $111.64                                         | Fiscal Q3 2027 beat                                                      |\n| GOOG (Alphabet/Google)       | —                                                         | Selected Blue Origin for AI satellite launch in 2029                     |\n\n---\n\n## Market Implications\n\nAnthropic&#x27;s IPO is a watershed moment for AI market structure, but Thursday&#x27;s price action delivered a cautionary message: even the world&#x27;s largest IPO couldn&#x27;t sustain a broad market rally. The Nasdaq&#x27;s intraday reversal from +1% to negative, combined with AMD&#x27;s sharp drop on China-linked scrutiny and Taiwan&#x27;s 4.6% plunge, suggests the AI trade is bifurcating — pure-play AI infrastructure names (storage, networking, cybersecurity) are finding buyers, while the broader semiconductor complex remains vulnerable to geopolitical and regulatory headwinds. Record-negative dealer gamma, as flagged in session analysis, is amplifying moves in both directions, making this an unusually volatile tape.\n\nThe cybersecurity sector stands out as a relative winner. Palo Alto&#x27;s raised guidance is being read as a demand signal for the entire sector, not just one company — and the JPMorgan/Visa/Mastercard vendor breach only reinforces enterprise urgency around security spending. Meanwhile, the UniCredit-Commerzbank deal clearing its political hurdle is a meaningful development for European banking consolidation, and Sanofi&#x27;s potential Insmed acquisition adds to a busy M&amp;A backdrop in pharma.\n\nLooking ahead to next week, the key risk signals are: (1) whether Anthropic stabilizes above its IPO price or becomes a cautionary tale about peak AI valuations; (2) the escalating US-China tech decoupling — TSMC restrictions, FCC bans, and the AMD short thesis all point to a tightening regulatory noose that could reprice China-exposed chip names; and (3) Intuit&#x27;s after-close report on Nov 19 as the next major earnings catalyst. The geopolitical wildcard — reports of potential US military action against Iran driving European gas prices higher — adds an energy cost dimension that could pressure margins across consumer and industrial sectors.\n\n---\n\n## Key Takeaways\n\n- 🟢 **Anthropic (ANT):** Largest IPO in history at $81.3B raised, $83/share price, $2T peak valuation — but retreated ~9% from open, a reminder that record IPOs don&#x27;t guarantee sustained gains.\n- 🔴 **Nasdaq:** Turned negative intraday despite Anthropic hype — the AI trade is not a rising tide lifting all boats right now.\n- 🔴 **AMD:** Down 8-9% on Hunterbrook Capital short report flagging China export risks — China-exposed chip names face a new layer of regulatory overhang.\n- 🟢 **PANW:** Raised annual forecast and beat across the board — cybersecurity demand is accelerating, and the whole sector is trading on that signal.\n- 🔴 **JPM/Visa/Mastercard:** Third-party vendor cyberattack leaked customer data — financial sector cyber risk is real and ongoing, even when the core systems aren&#x27;t breached.\n- 🔴 **Taiwan TAIEX:** -4.6% to a six-month low with chip/PCB names hitting limit-down — Asia&#x27;s AI selloff is not over.\n- 🟢 **Insmed (INSM):** +16-18% on Sanofi acquisition report — biotech M&amp;A is alive; rare disease assets command significant premiums.\n- 🟢 **GTA VI / Sony:** PlayStation captured ~75% of first-day GTA VI sales; Sony +3% — console hardware holders are the quiet winners of the gaming launch.\n- 🔴 **Take-Two (TTWO):** -2.7% on GTA VI launch day — classic &quot;sell the news&quot; after years of anticipation baked into the stock.\n- 🟢 **UniCredit/Commerzbank:** Broad agreement reached with German government — European banking M&amp;A is moving forward after a long political standoff.\n- 🔴 **Williams-Sonoma (WSM):** -8% on Q3 results — home goods spending remains under pressure even as broader consumer data holds up.\n- 🟢 **Walmart (WMT):** Raised annual targets ahead of holidays — the US consumer is spending, but fuel costs are a margin risk to watch.\n- 🟢 **Target (TGT):** Beat on every Q3 line including adj. EPS $2.65 — off-price and value retail is the sweet spot in this consumer environment.\n- 🔴 **European markets:** DAX -0.92%, CAC -0.81%, FTSE -0.85%, BEL20 -1.13% — AI optimism from Anthropic IPO couldn&#x27;t offset macro and defensive sector selling.\n- 🟡 **TSMC:** Tightening restrictions on Chinese chip designers as new US rules close a manufacturing loophole — supply chain risk for China AI hardware is rising.\n- 🟢 **Palo Alto CEO:** Attributed the wave of US network outages to a systemic infrastructure vulnerability — positions PANW as a solution provider, not just a beneficiary.\n- 🔴 **Novartis (NVS):** -2.4% after trimming medium-term guidance — even a 5% annual sales growth forecast through 2031 wasn&#x27;t enough to satisfy the market.\n- 🟢 **PDD Holdings:** +9.6% premarket on Q3 2026 beat — Chinese e-commerce is delivering results even amid macro uncertainty.\n- 🔴 **NetEase (NTES):** -10% on Q3 results — Chinese gaming/tech names remain a mixed bag; don&#x27;t paint the sector with one brush.\n- 🟡 **Intuit (INTU):** Reports after close today at $314.45 — next major earnings catalyst; watch for AI-driven guidance commentary on tax and financial software demand.\n\n---\n\n*Independent, data-driven signals.*  \n*No hype. No promotions. Just experimental market research from EverHint.*\n\n👉 **This is not financial advice. Market conditions change rapidly.**  \nDo your own due diligence.  \nSee [https://www.everhint.com/disclaimer/](https://www.everhint.com/disclaimer/) and [https://www.everhint.com/faqs/](https://www.everhint.com/faqs/)\n\n[/stock-market-news-november-18-2026-evening-update-last-12-hours-pacific-time/](https://www.everhint.com/stock-market-news-november-18-2026-evening-update-last-12-hours-pacific-time/)\n\n### Published by:\n\n[![Vladimir Shamgin](https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/14f44762bdbc3fa9b0a797cb8d19a003?s=